@meta
id: 01-04
title: The colon key is a place you go
chapter: 01
type: lesson
skills: cmdline, insert-i, esc, survival-motion
par: 16
minutes: 3
vscode: Ctrl-S, and the command palette
open: release-notes.md

@brief
`:wq` is not one key. It is three things: a colon that takes you to the
command line, a `w` that writes, and a `q` that quits. Once you see it
that way you can take it apart.

The colon puts the cursor on the last line of the screen, where vim keeps
its command line. That is a third mode. Everything typed there is a
command, not text, and nothing happens until you press Enter.

The first bullet in release-notes.md lost its `- ` marker. Put it back.
Then save the file with `:w` on its own, look at the file still sitting
there open, and only then quit with `:q`.

@keys
| `:` | go to the command line |
| `:w` | write the file, stay in it |
| `:q` | quit |
| `:wq` | both at once |
| `Esc` | abandon a command line you have started typing |

@hints
`j` moves down a line. The bullet you want is the first one under the
heading. `0` puts you in the first column if you have drifted right.
--
The marker is two characters, `-` and a space, and they go to the left of
what is already there. `i` inserts to the left.
--
`i- ` then `Esc`. Then `:w` and Enter — the file is written and you are
still in it. Then `:q` and Enter.

@solution
    j j i - <space> <Esc> :w<Enter> :q<Enter>

Writing and quitting are separate on purpose. Most of a day's vim is
`:w` — save, keep working, save again. `:q` is what you type when you are
finished with the file, which is much rarer.

Two things worth knowing now. A command line you have started and thought
better of is cancelled with `Esc`; nothing runs. And `:q` refuses to quit
when the file has unsaved changes — it stops you and says so. Vim is not
trying to trap you, it is trying not to lose your work. Lesson 01-12 is
about the way out.

@start release-notes.md
# Release 2.4.0

Refunds now respect the 30 day window
- Payment retries capped at three
- Customer emails moved to the queue

@expected release-notes.md
# Release 2.4.0

- Refunds now respect the 30 day window
- Payment retries capped at three
- Customer emails moved to the queue
